WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.912 --> 00:00:06.846
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:16.620
My name's Ralph Helmick,
and I'm a sculptor.

00:00:18.845 --> 00:00:20.220
I was asked if
there was some way

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.080
to create an artwork
that could help

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.650
guide a visitor from
the street level up

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:29.740
to the third floor main
entrance to the Institute.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:32.030
What's behind me is
what we ended up with.

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:36.075
100 gold leaf unique
sculptures of neurons.

00:00:36.075 --> 00:00:38.845
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:45.890
They're made out of a matrix
of stainless steel and bronze.

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:49.740
And they range in size from over
3 feet long to about 12 inches

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:56.370
tip to tip and suspended
very, very precisely.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:59.640
I came up with 5 different
cell bodies, different kinds

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:00.930
of dendritic forms.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.510
And so I made kits of
parts of different sizes

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:06.570
that I could then
customize and assemble.

00:01:06.570 --> 00:01:09.900
And I developed these
forms in consultation

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:10.920
with people at the lab.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.818
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:23.553
I chose gold leafing, because
it catches light so beautifully.

00:01:23.553 --> 00:01:24.970
And one of the
experimenters here,

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:28.530
they said they actually use
gold in one line of inquiry

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:31.590
in neuroscience.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:35.280
The title of this piece is
Schwerpunkt and that's a German

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:40.890
word one of the definitions
of which is 'focal point.'

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:42.990
When one views it
from a sweet spot that

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:46.560
is just as one's about to enter
the main office of the McGovern

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.500
Institute, one can look down
toward the entry and all

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:53.610
these hundred neurons
optically collapse

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:57.210
into a graphic image
of a brain hemisphere.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:00.000
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:04.140
That optical phenomenon
is called anamorphosis.

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:09.840
I think in Greek that translates
to reformation or re-creation.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:11.730
For me, this is a
metaphor for the discovery

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:13.690
that goes on in the building.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:16.320
Maybe a metaphor for
the elegant design

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.110
of many of the experiments
that are taking place here.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:26.820
What they do here is so
profound on a practical level,

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:30.480
and maybe in a way that
art is not practical.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.510
I hope that Schwerpunkt
could recall or evoke

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:34.710
that sense of discovery.

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:41.030
I think as a culture
we're hungry for surprise,

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:45.370
we're hungry for
optical phenomenon.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.480
We're hungry for a
positive experience.

00:02:51.163 --> 00:02:52.580
That's what I'm
trying to do here,

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:57.005
is something that elevates us.

00:02:57.005 --> 00:02:58.880
And I think every
researcher in this building

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.940
is doing research that seeks
to elevate our culture.

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:04.990
[MUSIC PLAYING]

